          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a heated lunch box take to heat food?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most take 30 to 60 minutes to bring a refrigerated meal to eating temperature, and longer for large, dense or very cold portions. Soups and saucy dishes heat fastest; dry leftovers and thick casseroles heat slowest. Frozen food can take hours and is a poor fit. Start heating about an hour before lunch and check the center of the food with a thermometer.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are cordless heated lunch boxes any good?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most are disappointing. A battery small enough to fit in a lunch box stores little energy, so cordless models heat slowly and often leave food warm rather than hot, and their batteries wear out. A better approach is a preheated vacuum food jar, or a corded lunch box powered by a portable battery pack with an AC outlet or 12-volt port.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use a 12V lunch box in a semi truck?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most North American heavy trucks use 12-volt systems, so a 12-volt lunch box usually works. Some trucks, especially in Europe and in certain fleets, run 24 volts, and a 12-volt-only box can be damaged or overheat on those. Check your truck's system voltage, and if it is 24 volts, buy a model with a dedicated 24-volt cord.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it safe to leave food in a heated lunch box all morning?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Perishable food should not spend more than two hours between 40°F and 140°F, or one hour in hot weather above 90°F. Keep food cold in a fridge or cooler until about an hour before lunch, then heat it. Do not plug in a cold meal at the start of a shift and leave it warming until noon.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What temperature should a heated lunch box reach?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                USDA guidance is to reheat leftovers to an internal temperature of 165°F. Some lunch box warmers bring food to a comfortable eating temperature that may fall short of that, especially with large or dense portions. Use an instant-read thermometer in the center of the food. If it is short, give it more time, stir if you can, or use a hotter appliance.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a heated lunch box drain my car battery?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A typical lunch box warmer draws only a few amps at 12 volts, so an hour of heating will not drain a healthy battery. Higher-draw 12-volt ovens, long engine-off stops and other accessories like a fridge add up, though. Run warmers with the engine on when possible, or use a socket that switches off with the ignition.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I clean an electric lunch box?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Unplug it and let it cool. Remove the inner container and lid and wash them in hot soapy water, or in the dishwasher if the manual says they are dishwasher-safe. Wipe the heating base with a damp cloth and never submerge it. Remove and wash silicone gaskets regularly, wash the container the same day, and dry everything before storing.
              </p>
            </div>

